{
  "_comment": "Shared declarative CREATE INDEX fixture matrix (#998). Single source of truth for valid + invalid CreateIndex cases, consumed by BOTH the Rust test (create_index_matrix.rs → CreateIndex::try_build) and the TS test (create_index_matrix.test.ts → createIndex()). Supersedes the flat create_index_try_build_msgpack.json — the 6 original valid-shape hex strings are merged here (single source of truth).",
  "_key_order_note": "rmp_serde::to_vec_named emits map keys in Rust STRUCT DECLARATION order: create_index, table, fields, unique, sorted, repo, then optional fields (index_type, fts_tokenizer, fts_language, functional_op, functional_args, vector_dim, vector_metric, vector_quantization, include, if_not_exists) in declaration order. The TS builder (ddl.ts createIndex) inserts keys in this same order so @msgpack/msgpack.encode (JS insertion order) produces byte-identical output for all field types involved (strings, bools, uint — no floats).",
  "_check_order_note": "Rust (create_index.rs TryFrom<&CreateIndex> for IndexSpec) and TS (ddl.ts createIndex()) run their rejection checks in a DIFFERENT order: TS checks unique+sorted (UniqueAndSorted) FIRST, before its numbered checks (so it wins over e.g. EmptyFields on a multi-violation input), while Rust checks EmptyFields first (check 1) and UniqueAndSorted last (check 10, after the btree-family checks). No case in this matrix currently triggers two simultaneous violations, so no case exposes this divergence — but a future multi-violation case could get a different `reason_contains` winner in each language. Informational only; the check order is intentionally left unreconciled here.",
  "_value_notes": {
    "discriminator": "BatchOp::CreateIndex(CreateIndexOp) serializes as a single-key map — the first struct field create_index: String is the discriminator, and BatchOp's custom Serialize forwards directly to CreateIndexOp::serialize. So the wire bytes for BatchOp::CreateIndex(op) and CreateIndexOp are identical.",
    "bool_defaults": "unique=false → 0xc2 (msgpack false), NOT absent. CreateIndexOp uses #[serde(default)] for the bool fields, which means serde writes the default value (false) as msgpack false. Only if_not_exists uses skip_serializing_if=is_false (absent when false, 0xc3 when true).",
    "vector_dim": "Values 128-65535 use uint16 (0xcd prefix). e.g. 384 = cd0180, 256 = cd0100.",
    "repo_default": "repo defaults to 'main' and is always present (#[serde(default = \"default_repo\")], no skip_serializing_if)."
  },
  "_consumer_notes": {
    "rust": "Load via concat!(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"), \"/tests/fixtures/create_index_matrix.json\"). Map each case's input to create_index(name, table) + fluent setters, call try_build(), assert accept/reject + wire_hex + reason_contains.",
    "ts": "Load via resolve(__dirname, '../../../../..', 'shamir-query-builder', 'tests', 'fixtures', 'create_index_matrix.json'). Map each case's input to createIndex(name, table, fields, opts), assert accept/reject + wire_hex + reason_contains."
  },
